# Expectation record for 12_preprocessor/003_bundled_header_inclusion.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 003_bundled_header_inclusion
area               = 12_preprocessor
description        = Inclusion of the compiler's own bundled freestanding headers, each exercised by a value or a relation it defines rather than merely by being named: stddef, stdint, stdarg, stdbool, limits, float, stdalign, stdnoreturn and iso646
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
HEADER EXCEPTION AND ITS REASON. This program takes the second of the corpus's two
sanctioned header exceptions and is the only program that takes it: see
tests/conformance/README.md, section "The two sanctioned header exceptions", under the
bolded paragraph beginning "Exception 2". It may include the nine REQUIRED bundled
freestanding headers -- stddef.h, stdint.h, stdarg.h, stdbool.h, limits.h, float.h,
stdalign.h, stdnoreturn.h and iso646.h -- and nothing else. Reason: it is the only place
the suite exercises include/ at all, and restricting it to <stdarg.h> as area 07 is
restricted would leave eight of the nine shipped headers included by nothing, which is a
coverage hole rather than a discipline. All nine are also freestanding headers the
reference compiler provides, which is what keeps the program compilable under both sides
of oracle (a). The bonus stdatomic.h is deliberately excluded even though the compiler
ships it: it is not among the nine required headers, no requirement mandates it, and
atomics can require -latomic, which is not in the shared flag set.

Free of undefined and unspecified behaviour by construction:
- No signed integer overflow. Every printed value is a small int, a fixed-width limit
  cast to int, or a 0/1 relation.
- No shift operator appears, so no shift count can be out of range.
- No pointer arithmetic and NO POINTER-TO-INTEGER CONVERSION of any kind. The null
  pointer is only ever compared, never printed or converted.
- No aliasing violation. The only object written after its declaration is the static
  alignas-qualified array aligned_buf, whose two unsigned char stores are separate
  statements and are read back in a later statement, never in the expression that wrote
  them.
- No read of uninitialized storage. The static object has static storage duration and is
  therefore zero-initialized before either store; both bytes that are printed are stored
  first.
- The variadic helper is exercised end to end and correctly: va_copy is taken before the
  original list has been read, so both lists start at the same position; each list is
  created or copied exactly once and ended exactly once in the same function; the second
  traversal reads the copy, never the exhausted original; and neither list is read past
  the number of arguments actually supplied.
- No object is modified twice between sequence points, and nothing depends on
  unspecified evaluation order: every call passes at most one side-effecting argument.
- offsetof is applied only to a struct with int8_t and int32_t members, never one
  containing int64_t, because the i386 System V ABI aligns int64_t to 4 inside a struct
  while the other three targets align it to 8, so the offsets are 4 and 8 on every
  target and can be asserted exactly.
- Hermetic, as constraint C4 requires: every input is a literal in the source. Nothing
  is opened, no socket is created, no environment variable and no program argument is
  read, and no storage is obtained from the heap.
- main returns 0, inside the 0-125 range the suite requires, so no exit status is
  truncated by the operating system.
- Verified empirically on this branch: the default strict warning gate produces zero
  diagnostics at -O0, -O1 and -O2, and the sanitizer gate runs to completion with empty
  stderr and exit status 0.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
ALIGNMENT IS OBSERVED THROUGH A LANGUAGE PROPERTY, NOT THROUGH AN ADDRESS, and this is
the one implementation-defined hazard this program had to be redesigned around. The
alignas specifier is applied to a struct MEMBER, so the requirement it imposes becomes a
property of a TYPE, and alignof reports that property directly: C11 6.7.5p2 makes 8 the
member's alignment requirement, a structure's requirement is at least that of each of
its members, and C11 6.2.8p1 makes every alignment a power of two, so
alignof(struct AlignedHolder) >= 8 holds BY THE LANGUAGE rather than by any target ABI.
EVERY ONE OF THE THREE ALIGNMENT FACTS IS PRINTED AS A RELATION RATHER THAN AS AN EXACT
VALUE, and that is what the language actually guarantees: an implementation may align more
strictly than requested, and 6.7.2.1p15 lets it insert additional padding between members,
so alignof >= 8, offsetof nonzero and divisible by 8, and sizeof divisible by 8 are
guaranteed while alignof == 8 and offsetof == 8 are not. The relations still fail exactly
when the request is ignored -- the member would then sit at offset 1, which is neither
divisible by 8 nor consistent with a structure alignment of 8 -- so nothing is given up by
declining to pin the padding this implementation happened to choose. An earlier form of this check converted the object's address to uintptr_t
and asserted its residue modulo 8 was zero, justified by uintptr_t being pointer-width
on every target so that the conversion crossed no width boundary. Equal width does not
carry that conclusion: C11 7.20.1.4p1 and 6.3.2.3p6 guarantee only that a pointer
converted to uintptr_t and back COMPARES EQUAL to the original, never that the integer's
low bits are the address's low bits. Whether a residue reflects alignment is a property
of the target ABI, not of the header set this program is testing, so the residue has
been removed rather than defended with an ABI citation. Nothing is lost: alignas and
alignof are both still exercised, the alignas-qualified storage is still written and
read back, and address-residue observation is owned by
10_declarations_and_types/007_alignof_alignas.c, whose subject that is and which carries
the two-step pointer-carrier discipline the observation needs.

WIDTH NORMALIZATION is the other central constraint. The four targets do not agree on
pointer or long width -- i686 has a 4-byte pointer and 4-byte long while x86_64, aarch64
and riscv64 have 8 of each (docs/technical-specifications.md lines 457-462) -- and
size_t, ptrdiff_t and intptr_t follow the pointer. Every fact that depends on one of
those widths is therefore printed ONLY as a 0/1 relation (size_t_eq_ptr, ptrdiff_eq_ptr,
intptr_eq_ptr) or as an inequality with margin (int_max_ge_32767, dbl_dig_ge_10). Each
relation is 1 on all four targets precisely BECAUSE the widths move together, so the
line still carries a real claim about the headers while staying byte-identical
everywhere. No pointer-width or long-width quantity is printed as a value anywhere.

Plain char signedness (measured signed on x86_64 and i686, unsigned on aarch64 and
riscv64) is avoided by reading the character limits through SCHAR_MIN and SCHAR_MAX and
fixed-width values through int8_t. long double is absent entirely, its representation
having been measured at 16, 12, 16 and 16 bytes across the four targets; that type
belongs to 13_floating_point/004_long_double_target_restricted.

No target is dropped, no oracle is disabled and no marker is carried. Measured on this
branch with gcc 13.4.0 and the matching cross drivers under QEMU user-mode 10.1.0:
stdout is byte-identical and the exit status is 0 in all twelve cells.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
char_bit=8
schar_min=-128 schar_max=127
int8_min=-128 int8_max=127
uint16_max=65535
sizeof_int32=4 sizeof_int64=8
alignof_int32=4 alignof_int8=1
offsetof_b=4 offsetof_c=8
size_t_eq_ptr=1
ptrdiff_eq_ptr=1
intptr_eq_ptr=1
int_max_ge_32767=1
null_compares_equal=1
bool_true=1 bool_iso646_and=1
iso646_macros_present=1
flt_radix=2 dbl_dig_ge_10=1
alignas_type_alignment=1
alignas_member_offset=1
alignas_size_multiple=1
alignas_object_roundtrip=1
noreturn_macro_present=1
varargs_two_passes=120
END
